WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.290
What would you think if the thermometer read minus one hundred kelvin?

2
00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:13.060
You can't go below absolute zero — but that number doesn't mean going down there at all: it means crossing over to the other side of infinite temperature.

3
00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:17.510
And a gas that has crossed to that side is hotter than anything you're holding.

4
00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:31.280
The headline gets written like this: scientists have gone below absolute zero.

5
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.570
The sentence sounds like a record, like a limit being broken.

6
00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:37.780
But that is not what happened.

7
00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:46.390
What happened is stranger, and more interesting: a temperature with a minus sign in front of it is not the bottom of cold, it is the top of hot.

8
00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:51.480
A gas sitting at minus one nanokelvin counts as hotter than the core of the sun.

9
00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:59.130
To understand that, you have to break the thermometer open, look inside, and ask what that number is actually measuring.

10
00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:03.420
We are used to thinking of temperature as the amount of heat in a body.

11
00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.150
It isn't.

12
00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:08.320
Temperature is not a quantity, it is a slope.

13
00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:15.330
It is the ratio that tells you how much a system's disorder, its entropy, grows when you add a little energy to it.

14
00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:22.820
The physicist's definition is exactly that: the inverse of temperature is the rate at which entropy changes with energy.

15
00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:27.430
It sounds dry, but our entire everyday experience follows from it.

16
00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:35.240
When you put your hand into hot tea, energy flows from the tea into your hand, because that transfer makes the total entropy larger.

17
00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:44.170
When the tea and your hand come to the same temperature the flow stops, because from then on, whichever way you carried the energy, the total disorder would go down.

18
00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:49.740
Heat flowing from hot to cold is not a law; it is the outcome of bookkeeping.

19
00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:55.950
Now ask this question: when you add energy to a system, does its entropy always increase?

20
00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.800
In ordinary matter it does.

21
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:08.050
Heat a container of gas and the atoms move faster, the number of velocity states they can reach grows, the number of ways they can be arranged multiplies.

22
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:10.260
Entropy climbs.

23
00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:14.550
And there is no end to it, because kinetic energy has no ceiling.

24
00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.720
You can always make an atom go a little faster.

25
00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:23.450
And because there is no ceiling, the temperature of such systems always carries a plus sign.

26
00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:26.940
That is why the thermometer is a trustworthy instrument.

27
00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:30.430
Can we imagine a system that does have a ceiling?

28
00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:34.080
We can, and in fact nature is full of them.

29
00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:37.730
Take an atomic nucleus placed in a magnetic field.

30
00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:49.540
The spin of the nucleus behaves like a compass needle; in the simplest case it has only two options: pointing along the field, which is the low energy state, or pointing against it, which is the high energy state.

31
00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:53.190
There is no third option, no higher rung above that.

32
00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:56.040
The energy ladder has a top.

33
00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:59.530
Imagine billions of such needles inside a crystal.

34
00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:10.540
If they are all lined up parallel to the field, the system is at its lowest energy and there is exactly one way to do that; entropy is zero, and the temperature is plus zero kelvin.

35
00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:18.670
Now start feeding in energy: some needles flip over, and because there are countless ways of choosing which ones flipped, entropy grows quickly.

36
00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:21.360
Familiar so far.

37
00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:30.930
The critical point lies exactly halfway: when half the needles point up and half point down, the number of ways to arrange the system reaches its largest value.

38
00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:33.460
Entropy is at its peak.

39
00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:40.950
Here, raising the energy a little contributes nothing at all to entropy, the slope is zero, which means the temperature is infinite.

40
00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.640
Nothing strange has happened yet.

41
00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:47.130
The strange thing happens at the next step.

42
00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:56.060
If you push past the halfway mark and force the majority of the needles to point the wrong way, the system gains energy again, but its options begin to shrink.

43
00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:03.230
In the state where every single one of them has flipped, there is once again only one arrangement, and entropy falls back to zero.

44
00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:06.720
So energy is rising while entropy is falling.

45
00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:08.930
The slope is negative.

46
00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:14.020
And temperature, being the inverse of that slope, carries a minus sign.

47
00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:25.990
The order of the rungs on this ladder runs like this: you start at plus zero kelvin, you warm up, exactly at the halfway point the temperature flies off to infinity, from there it passes over to minus infinity,

48
00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:31.080
and as you keep filling the system you climb from minus infinity up toward minus zero.

49
00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:37.610
A fully inverted system is at minus zero kelvin, and that is the hottest condition that system can reach.

50
00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.140
Why the hottest?

51
00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:44.270
Because temperature is the thing that decides which way energy will flow.

52
00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:51.600
Whatever you bring an inverted system into contact with, energy flows out of it; because losing energy makes its entropy larger.

53
00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.050
There is nothing it cannot give away, and nothing it can take in.

54
00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:59.540
It gives heat to every system at positive temperature.

55
00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:04.790
It gives heat to a thousand degree furnace, and it gives heat to the center of the sun.

56
00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:07.800
It is hotter than infinite temperature.

57
00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:12.730
The irritating part of all this is that it is really a defect of temperature itself.

58
00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:25.020
When physicists use, instead of temperature, its inverse, the entropy slope per unit of energy, the weirdness evaporates: that quantity moves from the coldest to the hottest without a single break, smoothly,

59
00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:29.310
descending from positive to zero and from zero into negative.

60
00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:32.160
Nowhere does it fly off to infinity.

61
00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:37.890
So minus kelvin is not a paradox of nature, it is the shadow of a badly chosen coordinate.

62
00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:41.380
This line of reasoning stayed on the blackboard for a long time.

63
00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:47.910
Someone had to turn it into something on a tabletop, into an apparatus built out of cold metal and copper wire.

64
00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:57.800
In January of nineteen fifty-one, Edward Purcell and Robert Pound of Harvard University published a two page paper in the American physics journal Physical Review.

65
00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:02.730
The title was plain: a nuclear spin system at negative temperature.

66
00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:05.420
Pound was the one who found the key.

67
00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:16.910
In sufficiently pure crystals of lithium fluoride, the nuclear spins were taking a surprisingly long time to exchange energy with the crystal lattice around them: on the order of minutes at room temperature,

68
00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:18.960
roughly five minutes.

69
00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:27.570
The spins talking to one another, by contrast, feeling each other's magnetic fields and settling into a common equilibrium, took microseconds.

70
00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:34.420
Those two clocks running so differently may look like a dry technical detail, but it was the thing that made everything possible.

71
00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:43.830
Because the population of spins was behaving like an independent system, effectively insulated from the crystal it sat in and brought into equilibrium within itself.

72
00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:46.680
Which means it could have a temperature of its own.

73
00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.370
A temperature different from the crystal's.

74
00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:58.460
The setup was this: they put the crystal inside a powerful magnet and waited for the spins to line up parallel to the field, to become cold and orderly.

75
00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:04.670
Then they reversed the direction of the field, fast enough that the spins had no chance to follow it around.

76
00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:09.120
The spins stayed where they were; the field turned.

77
00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:15.010
In an instant all those needles were pointing the wrong way, the high energy way.

78
00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:17.700
The inverted population had been built.

79
00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:21.350
And how did they actually see that this had happened?

80
00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:23.560
This is the most elegant part.

81
00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:31.850
An ordinary sample, held in a radio wave at the right frequency, absorbs energy; a trough of absorption appears on the measuring instrument.

82
00:07:31.850 --> 00:07:38.540
The sample of Purcell and Pound did the exact opposite: it gave energy to the radio circuit.

83
00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:41.550
The trough turned into a peak.

84
00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:44.880
The system was not listening, it was speaking.

85
00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:56.050
And over the minutes that followed, as the exchange with the crystal lattice slowly went forward, the spin population came back from negative temperatures; first it passed through infinite temperature,

86
00:07:56.050 --> 00:08:02.900
that intermediate condition in which it is spread out completely at random, and then it settled into ordinary positive temperature.

87
00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:12.790
In their paper the two of them wrote plainly that this condition is not coldness but extreme heat, and that such a system would transfer energy to any system at positive temperature.

88
00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:18.680
Five years later, in nineteen fifty-six, Norman Ramsey closed the theoretical accounts.

89
00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:28.890
Ramsey showed that negative temperature does not break the laws of thermodynamics, it only requires stating them more carefully, and he tied the validity of the concept to three conditions.

90
00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:37.340
First, the energy levels of the system must be bounded from above; in a system without a ceiling there can be no such thing as a negative temperature.

91
00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:44.670
Second, the system must have come to equilibrium within itself, otherwise assigning it a single temperature is meaningless.

92
00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:52.960
Third, the system must be sufficiently insulated from systems that have no ceiling, from the crystal it sits in, from the container, from the world.

93
00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:58.370
The long relaxation time of lithium fluoride was buying exactly that third condition.

94
00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:05.700
The moment those conditions were met, negative temperature stopped being a laboratory curiosity and walked into engineering.

95
00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:10.470
Because an inverted population is the operating principle of the laser itself.

96
00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:20.200
What you do in a laser is pump most of the atoms in a medium into the excited, high energy state, which is to say, invert the distribution across those energy levels.

97
00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:26.090
The instant the distribution is inverted, the medium stops absorbing light and starts multiplying it.

98
00:09:26.090 --> 00:09:37.260
The maser that Charles Townes and his co-workers built with microwaves in nineteen fifty-four, the first laser that Theodore Maiman made out of ruby in nineteen sixty, all of it rests on that inversion.

99
00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:44.910
In the early years these media were called negative temperature media outright in the literature, because technically that is exactly what they are.

100
00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:52.080
Inside the laser pointer on your desk there is a small region sitting at minus kelvin with respect to one particular degree of freedom.

101
00:09:52.080 --> 00:10:01.650
The same language is still in working order in the world of magnetic resonance; spin temperature is an everyday calculating tool in imaging and polarization techniques.

102
00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:07.220
For sixty years negative temperature lived in the same two places: in spins and in light.

103
00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:12.150
What those two had in common was that their energy ladders were naturally short.

104
00:10:12.150 --> 00:10:15.800
The hard question was this: can motion itself do it?

105
00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:19.290
Can you put a ceiling on the kinetic energy of a gas?

106
00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:24.220
In January of twenty thirteen the answer came from Munich, and it was yes.

107
00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:33.470
A team that included Ulrich Schneider, Immanuel Bloch and Simon Braun, from Ludwig Maximilian University and the Max Planck Institute of Quantum Optics,

108
00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:40.320
announced in the journal Science that they had produced a negative absolute temperature for the motional degree of freedom of atoms.

109
00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:43.650
The method was to create a ceiling out of nothing.

110
00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:54.180
They placed a Bose-Einstein condensate made of roughly a hundred thousand potassium thirty-nine atoms into an optical lattice, an artificial crystal, built out of intersecting laser beams.

111
00:10:54.180 --> 00:11:03.590
The kinetic energy of a particle inside a crystal is not unbounded the way a free atom's is; it is squeezed into an energy band, and a band has a top as well.

112
00:11:03.590 --> 00:11:06.120
The ceiling had arrived.

113
00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:08.810
But one ceiling was not enough.

114
00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:12.780
Every energy account of the system had to be bounded from above.

115
00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:19.630
To limit the energy coming from the atoms interacting with one another, they turned the interaction from repulsive to attractive.

116
00:11:19.630 --> 00:11:26.640
To limit the energy coming from the trap holding the atoms together, they inverted the trap itself: the valley turned into a hill.

117
00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:31.250
Once all three accounts had been capped, they tipped the distribution over.

118
00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:39.220
The result was directly visible in the measurement: the atoms were piled up not at the lowest energy of the band but in the highest momentum state.

119
00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:43.510
A temperature with a minus sign, on the order of nanokelvin.

120
00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:47.480
And this state stayed on its feet for hundreds of milliseconds.

121
00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:50.810
The most surprising result was a by-product.

122
00:11:50.810 --> 00:11:57.660
A gas with attractive interactions normally collapses in on itself; negative pressure tries to pull it together.

123
00:11:57.660 --> 00:12:00.830
The gas in Munich did not collapse.

124
00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:05.280
Being at negative temperature made it stable against collapse.

125
00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:12.770
The team noted that this situation carries a formal resemblance to the negative pressure attributed to dark energy in cosmology.

126
00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:18.340
Here one has to be careful: this is an analogy, a surface kinship between equations.

127
00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:26.790
It is not evidence that dark energy is a system at negative temperature, it is speculation, and it is not part of the consensus in that field.

128
00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:31.880
After this, another prediction that had been standing all alone was confirmed as well.

129
00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:43.530
In nineteen forty-nine Lars Onsager studied the vortices in a two dimensional fluid statistically and showed that the phase space of such a system is bounded, and that negative temperature states are therefore possible.

130
00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:55.500
What Onsager predicted was strange: at high energy, which is to say at negative temperature, vortices turning in the same direction cluster together instead of dispersing, merging into giant vortices.

131
00:12:55.500 --> 00:13:06.030
In twenty nineteen, two studies published side by side in the same issue of Science, one from the University of Queensland, the other from the University of Otago in New Zealand,

132
00:13:06.030 --> 00:13:15.600
observed exactly that in two dimensional condensates: large vortex clusters, characterized by negative temperature, arising spontaneously out of turbulence.

133
00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:20.530
A calculation that had waited seventy years on the shelf was closed.

134
00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:25.780
And precisely at this point, the harshest objection in the story arrives.

135
00:13:25.780 --> 00:13:36.950
In twenty fourteen, Jörn Dunkel and Stefan Hilbert published a paper in the journal Nature Physics with a title that lands like a slap: consistent thermostatistics forbids negative absolute temperatures.

136
00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:49.240
Their thesis was this: the definition of entropy that has been in use for sixty years, Boltzmann's definition, produces inconsistencies; the older volume entropy of Gibbs should be used in its place.

137
00:13:49.240 --> 00:13:54.490
And when the calculation is done with that definition, temperature never comes out negative.

138
00:13:54.490 --> 00:13:59.740
This is not an argument about experiments, it is an argument about bookkeeping.

139
00:13:59.740 --> 00:14:09.310
The fact that the sample of Purcell and Pound gave energy to the radio circuit, the fact that the atoms in Munich piled up at the top of the band, neither of those is in dispute.

140
00:14:09.310 --> 00:14:13.920
What is in dispute is which number you write down for that condition.

141
00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:16.450
The replies did not take long.

142
00:14:16.450 --> 00:14:27.300
Daan Frenkel and Patrick Warren argued that the Gibbs definition fails to meet the most basic requirement of thermodynamics: two bodies that have come to equilibrium must show the same temperature;

143
00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:31.750
Gibbs entropy does not provide that, and Boltzmann entropy does.

144
00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:38.280
The Munich team answered the objection with a comment of its own, and Dunkel and Hilbert replied to both.

145
00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:47.850
At the formal level the argument has not closed; but the great majority of physicists go on using the Boltzmann definition and the language of negative temperature in practice.

146
00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:53.900
What this quarrel really teaches, I think, is that temperature is not a property of a body.

147
00:14:53.900 --> 00:14:59.790
The fundamental quantity in thermodynamics is entropy; temperature is derived from it.

148
00:14:59.790 --> 00:15:04.560
Change the way you count entropy and what the thermometer is telling you changes too.

149
00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:15.890
For a glass of tea, a star, or the air in a room, the difference between the two definitions is too small to be measured; they only begin to part company in systems that are small and have a ceiling.

150
00:15:15.890 --> 00:15:20.820
Which is to say, the disagreement sits exactly where the strangeness lives.

151
00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:24.950
The legacy that remains in practice is sturdier.

152
00:15:24.950 --> 00:15:31.960
Temperature is not a single number belonging to an object, it is a property of one particular degree of freedom of that object.

153
00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:38.490
The spins inside a crystal can sit at a negative temperature while the lattice that carries them is at room temperature.

154
00:15:38.490 --> 00:15:44.540
This is not an evasion; the word meant this from the beginning, and we simply had no need to notice.

155
00:15:44.540 --> 00:15:51.710
And one more thing remains: reaching minus zero kelvin is as impossible as reaching plus zero kelvin.

156
00:15:51.710 --> 00:15:55.360
Both ends are approached, never arrived at.

157
00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:59.170
Because there is no such place as below absolute zero.

158
00:15:59.170 --> 00:16:05.220
The road to negative temperature does not pass through zero, it goes around by way of infinity.

159
00:16:05.220 --> 00:16:08.710
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

160
00:16:08.710 --> 00:16:12.200
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
